description:
`VirtualFileSystemImpl` forwarded every call to `createVirtualFileSystem()` and nothing else:

```ts
/**
 * @deprecated Use createVirtualFileSystem() factory function instead.
 */
export class VirtualFileSystemImpl implements VirtualFileSystem {
  private readonly vfs: VirtualFileSystem

  constructor(enableDebugLogging = false) {
    this.vfs = createVirtualFileSystem(enableDebugLogging)
  }

  getCurrentDirectory(): string {
    return this.vfs.getCurrentDirectory()
  }
  // ... 11 more, all identical
}
```

All 12 methods were one-line passthroughs — verified individually, no added logic anywhere. It was marked deprecated when the factory was introduced, but nothing ever migrated: the worker and every test still constructed the wrapper.

`moo-dang` is a private app with no external consumers, so the compatibility shim had nobody to be compatible with.

## Change

Replaces `new VirtualFileSystemImpl(x)` with `createVirtualFileSystem(x)` at all six call sites — `workers/shell.worker.ts` plus five test files — and deletes the class.

One wrinkle worth noting: the `VirtualFileSystem` interface is declared in `shell/types.ts`, not re-exported from `virtual-file-system.ts`. Typing the call sites against the interface therefore required importing it from `./types`, not from the module the factory lives in.

`virtual-file-system.test.ts`'s `describe` block is renamed from `VirtualFileSystemImpl` to `createVirtualFileSystem`. Every assertion is unchanged — only construction and the block name differ.

`job-controller.test.ts` reaches the module through a dynamic `await import('./virtual-file-system')`, so its destructured binding was updated rather than an import statement.

No `instanceof VirtualFileSystemImpl` existed anywhere, and the class was not re-exported from any barrel, so nothing depended on class identity.

